      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 by the American Academy of Pediatrics.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "CONTEXT",
            "content": "Brain injury is common in preterm infants, and predictors of neurodevelopmental outcome are relevant."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To assess the prognostic test accuracy of the background activity of the EEG recorded as amplitude-integrated EEG (aEEG) or conventional EEG early in life in preterm infants for predicting neurodevelopmental outcome."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
            "content": "The Cochrane Library, PubMed, Embase, and the Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY SELECTION",
            "content": "We included observational studies that had obtained an aEEG or EEG within 7 days of life in preterm infants and reported neurodevelopmental outcomes 1 to 10 years later."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DATA EXTRACTION",
            "content": "Two reviewers independently performed data extraction with regard to participants, prognostic testing, and outcomes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Thirteen observational studies with a total of 1181 infants were included. A meta-analysis was performed based on 3 studies (267 infants). Any aEEG background abnormality was a predictor of abnormal outcome. For prediction of a developmental quotient <70 points, cerebral palsy, or death, the pooled sensitivity was 0.83 (95% confidence interval, 0.69-0.92) and specificity 0.83 (95% confidence interval, 0.77-0.87)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "LIMITATIONS",
            "content": "All studies were at high risk of bias. Heterogeneity was evident among the studies with regard to the investigated aEEG and EEG variables, neurodevelopmental outcomes, and cutoff values."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "aEEG or EEG recorded within the first 7 days of life in preterm infants may have potential as a predictor for later neurodevelopmental outcome. We need high-quality studies to confirm these findings. Meanwhile, the prognostic value of aEEG and EEG should be used only as a scientific tool."
          }
        ]
      },
